parts.text:
Right — and the compounding is worth stating precisely, because it's bounded and then it collapses:

- **Per method call**, the tax is ~2 ns × (number of static reads). A method reading four constants through `this.self` pays ~8 ns. That's still invisible outside a loop — but it's the *shape* of a problem, and shapes get copied.
- **The hoist doesn't just cap it — it inverts it.** `const self = this.self;` pays the 2 ns *once*, and every subsequent read benched at 0.37 ns — cheaper than even the inline cast (4 ns), because V8 treats the hoisted class as a plain constant while `this.constructor` re-reads the property each time. So a multi-constant method with hoisted `self` is *faster* than today's 103-cast style, not just prettier.

Which suggests the mandate should make the hoist the canonical form rather than a hot-loop footnote:

```ts
showTooltip() {
  const self = this.self; // one line, one cast site, zero repeated tax
  this.dwellSeconds.value = self.TOOLTIP_DWELL_SECONDS;
  this.delaySeconds.value = self.TOOLTIP_DELAY_SECONDS;
}
```

Rule of thumb that falls out cleanly: **one static read → `this.self.X` inline; two or more, or any loop → hoist `self` as the first line.** That's the same graduation the docs already teach for methods and refs in hot loops, so it's not a new idea to learn — it's the existing hoisting doctrine applied to one more derivation. And it reads better anyway: `self.TOOLTIP_DWELL_SECONDS` is the closest JavaScript gets to Python's `type(self)` ergonomics with the cast paid exactly once per class.

Ready to encode it on your word: skill section (the getter form, the cast-to-`$Class` rule, the one-read/many-reads graduation with the measured numbers, the `self` vs `Namespace.Class` staleness warning), mirror resync, and the `$Tooltip` teaching examples flipped to the hoisted form.

